WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.660
Configuration as code means important settings are defined in version-controlled, reviewable artifacts rather than changed only by hand.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:18.600
Infrastructure-as-code, declarative deployment files, policy definitions, application configuration, and environment templates can all participate.

3
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.780
The security value is not that every setting becomes perfect.

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.480
The value is that changes become visible, repeatable, auditable, and easier to reason about.

5
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:36.280
Peer review and pull requests bring configuration changes into the same engineering workflow as code changes.

6
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:44.940
Reviewers can see when a firewall rule is opened, a permission is broadened, a logging setting changes, or a new feature flag affects authorization behavior.

7
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.160
Change history helps teams understand when a setting changed and why.

8
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.980
That history is invaluable during incident response and troubleshooting.

9
00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:01.580
Reproducible environments reduce the gap between what teams think is deployed and what is actually running.

10
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:09.520
If configuration is declarative, a new environment can be created from known inputs rather than accumulated manual changes.

11
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:20.180
Policy-as-code at a high level can add automated checks for risky patterns, such as public storage, broad IAM, missing encryption, or unsafe network exposure.

12
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:29.020
Manual console changes should be controlled because they can bypass review, create drift, and make environments difficult to reproduce.

13
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:36.560
Sometimes emergency changes are necessary, but they should be captured afterward, reviewed, and reconciled back into the source of truth.

14
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:44.740
Otherwise the console becomes the real configuration system while the repository slowly becomes documentation that may or may not be true.
